            <ArticleTitle>Clinical Case of the Month: Hypereosinophilia in a Young Woman with a History of Childhood Asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>205-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hypereosinophillia is a rare clinical entity. It is associated with a wide differential diagnosis including neoplasm, infection, and allergic etiologies. Clinicians should have a well defined approach to hypereosinophilia in order to find treatable causes. We present a case of hypereosinophillia caused by parasitic infection with Toxocara canis. We also review epidemiology, transmission, microbiology, and management of Toxocara canis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
